sleeping evil long-lost heroes and a  world without wizards the rings of power  is middle-earth like you've never seen  it before  amazon studio's highly anticipated  series the lord of the rings the rings  of power promises to be an exciting new  chapter of on-screen middle-earth  adventures the show has been in  development for years ever since the  studio bought the rights to portions of  the lord of the rings and the hobbit  back in late 2017.  since then untested yet inspired  showrunners jd payne and patrick mckay  promised to dive deep into middle-earth  lore by exploring a critical part in  tolkien's legendarium events surrounding  the forging of the rings of power  [Music]  the sweeping setting will go far beyond  the mere creation of overpowered jewelry  though promotional material has  consistently hinted at a vast saga that  stretches from the powerful island  nation of numenor in the west to the icy  waste of the forward wait in the north  to the toasty southlands and beyond the  ability to balance multiple story arcs  set across such a large area will be one  of the showrunner's biggest tasks  far-reaching geography isn't the only  challenge though time is another concern  tolkien's world is known for its  complexity and richness much of which  comes from its sprawling intricate  history which the author mapped out for  tens of thousands of years in the books  the events depicted in the show were  supposed to occur over a sweeping 3 500  year stretch of middle earth history in  comparison the bulk of the lord of the  rings story takes place within the short  and sweet time span of a single year  hobbit occurs in a similarly short time  frame so when exactly does this massive  rings of power timeline take place and  how will it fit into the lord of the  rings history that we all know and love  well we've known the chronological  setting of the rings of power for years  now back in early 2019 the show's  official twitter page announced that it  would be focusing on the second age of  middle-earth this was exciting and  nerve-wracking news for tolkien fandom  who had never before experienced an  on-screen adaptation of this early era  of tolkien's world in case you're  feeling a little lost here are some  basic facts the second age is nearly 3  500 years long unsurprisingly it comes  before the third age and after the  cataclysmic events of the first stage in  fact the events of the rings of power  will be playing out somewhere between 3  000 and 6 500 years before anything that  takes place in the lord of the rings  this means a lot for the way middle  earth will look in the show for instance  the resurgent sauron won't be around  just yet while certainly alive  spiritually gandalf and the other istari  won't quite be around in their incarnate  wizarding forms there isn't a balrog in  moria and mordor won't yet exist there  will be a few familiar faces though  characters such as galadriel and elrond  will play vital roles in the show and at  some point or another we'll get to  revisit regions such as rivendell and  kaza doom and then there's sauron  himself whose terrible influence on  middle earth will only grow as the  series draws on some aspects of the show  might be unfamiliar even to fans of the  books one of the biggest changes in this  sense has to do with the timeline in an  interview with vanity fair in february  2022 it was revealed that the story was  going to condense the entire timeline of  the second age into a single point in  time the reason for this staggering  change is to avoid a ton of cast  turnover with the show's mortal  characters while this makes sense from a  practical perspective it does present a  few issues tolkien strikes a remarkable  cadence in his storytelling in spite of  the fact that it takes place over  hundreds of years the immense span  allows him to set up high stakes that  will be hard to replicate in a single  story patrick mckay and jd payne seem to  think it's possible to pull this off  however and they even have the backing  of the tolkien estate for their plans  can they really manage it that's a  question that only time can answer  [Music]
 

